    "summary": "Pigpen‑heavy acoustic set, Attics Of My Life & Turn On Your Lovelight shine",
    "blurb": "The show opens with a tight acoustic Pigpen‑driven first set featuring Attics Of My Life and How Long Blues. The electric second half builds to a soaring Turn On Your Lovelight and a rousing Dancin' In The Street, while the encore delivers Uncle John's Band. Overall performance is energetic and memorable despite modest recording fidelity.",
    "review": "On July 12, 1970 at the Fillmore East, the Dead delivered one of those rare Pigpen‑centric nights that still feels fresh. An acoustic first set led by Ron \"Pigpen\" McKernan roared through bluesy numbers like How Long Blues and the hauntingly beautiful Attics Of My Life, with Jerry slipping onto steel guitar for a brief New Riders interlude that kept the crowd buzzing into the early morning hours. The second set erupted with classic Dead power‑trading—China Cat Sunflower/I Know You Rider segued into high‑energy jams before culminating in a blistering Turn On Your Lovelight and a sing‑along Dancin' In The Street, capped off by an encore of Uncle John’s Band that left the audience humming as dawn broke. While the available audience tape is lo‑fi, with lobby chatter and occasional speed wobble, it remains listenable enough to capture the night’s magic, making this recording the go‑to source for anyone wanting to experience Pigpen’s soulful command and the band’s late‑summer fire.\n\nFans should note that the sound isn’t pristine—hand‑clapping and tape hiss are present—but the performance outweighs those flaws. This show stands out as a high‑point in the early ’70s era, offering both deep blues feeling and the electric exuberance that would define the Dead’s next decade.",
